import re, pathlib
P = pathlib.Path("src/main_arxiv.tex"); s = P.read_text()
def rep(old, new, cnt=1):
    global s
    pat = re.escape(old).replace('\\ ', r'\s+'); n = len(re.findall(pat, s)); assert n == cnt, (old[:70], n)
    s = re.sub(pat, lambda m: new, s)
# ---- Section 2 ----
rep("\\citet{rios2020optimal} survey the optimization models, and the location-allocation view of districting, in which centres are located and units allocated to them, goes back to \\citet{hess1965nonpartisan}.",
    "\\citet{rios2020optimal} survey the optimization models; the location-allocation view of districting goes back to \\citet{hess1965nonpartisan}.")
rep("Emergency medical services have a location literature of their own, surveyed by \\citet{brotcorne2003ambulance} and \\citet{aringhieri2017emergency}, in which stations are located and vehicles relocated against coverage targets; districting enters when every station is given a fixed catchment, which is the static view this paper takes.",
    "Emergency medical services have a location literature of their own~\\citep{brotcorne2003ambulance,aringhieri2017emergency}, in which stations are located and vehicles relocated against coverage targets; this paper takes the static view in which every station has a fixed catchment.")
rep("The multiscale merge-split sampler of \\citet{autry2021multiscale} represents a plan on two levels of geographic units, counties and precincts, proposes at the coarse level and refines at the fine level, and is reversible with a known target; it is the closest predecessor of the supergraph step of FalCom. The hierarchy there is one of units inside a fixed number of districts, whereas the hierarchy of FalCom is one of districts, each with a facility and a capacity, whose number is not fixed.",
    "The multiscale merge-split sampler of \\citet{autry2021multiscale}, which proposes on a coarse level of units and refines on a fine one with a known target, is the closest predecessor of the supergraph step of FalCom; its hierarchy is one of units inside a fixed number of districts, whereas FalCom's is one of districts with facilities and capacities, whose number is not fixed.")
rep("Coherence yields a nested spatial structure. The objectives are fixed-charge, median or covering. FalCom addresses the single-demand, single-flow, coherent case, the simplest non-trivial hierarchical setting. The model has no objective; the hierarchical median of Section~\\ref{sec:exp-milp} serves only as a benchmark energy and a readout. A level-2 facility in our model offers no service of its own: its capacity is the aggregate of the level-1 teams it coordinates, the nested structure of a regional management tier rather than a successively exclusive service hierarchy. The models of this literature are solved by mixed-integer programming and metaheuristics~\\citep{contreras2020hierarchical, darmian2021optimization, wang2025cluster}. Capacity makes them harder, as it already does in single-level location~\\citep{aardal1998reformulation}, because the capacity consumed at one level constrains what is feasible at the next. None of these methods samples the space of feasible hierarchical plans.",
    "Coherence yields a nested spatial structure. FalCom addresses the single-demand, single-flow, coherent case with no objective: a level-2 facility offers no service of its own, its capacity is the aggregate of the level-1 teams it coordinates, a regional management tier rather than a service hierarchy, and the hierarchical median of Section~\\ref{sec:exp-milp} serves only as a benchmark energy. The models of this literature are solved by mixed-integer programming and metaheuristics~\\citep{contreras2020hierarchical, darmian2021optimization, wang2025cluster}, capacity makes them harder~\\citep{aardal1998reformulation}, and none of them samples the space of feasible hierarchical plans.")
rep("and, within a sampler, a number of districts determined by capacity and demand rather than fixed in advance, which optimization models such as the max-$p$-regions problem of \\citet{duque2012maxp} already allow. Addressing them starts with a precise statement of the problem the chain must respect, which the next section develops.",
    "and a number of districts set by capacity and demand rather than fixed in advance, as the max-$p$-regions problem of \\citet{duque2012maxp} allows in optimization. The next section states the problem the chain must respect.")
# ---- 7.4 protocol ----
rep("The reference calibrates the noise of the statistic; it cannot detect a region of the state space that all four chains miss, and all four start from the same sector-wise construction, so start-independence is weaker evidence here than on the enumerated instances, where the starts were the first, middle and last feasible states in lexicographic order. Effective sample sizes per chain, on the per-step series, range from $426$ to $526$ for the cut-edge count and $395$ to $474$ for the station-leg energy, the two slowest observables, through $631$ to $795$ for the energy, to $2{,}200$ to $3{,}400$ for the number of Groups. Summed over the chains, the $6{,}400$ stored plans are worth about $2{,}970$ independent draws for the energy and $1{,}850$ for the cut-edge count, and the frequencies below are quoted to the precision this supports.",
    "The reference calibrates the noise of the statistic; it cannot detect a region of the state space that all chains miss, which is why a chain from a different construction is added below. Effective sample sizes per chain, on the per-step series, range from about $400$ for the cut-edge count and the station-leg energy, the two slowest observables, to $2{,}200$ to $3{,}400$ for the number of Groups, so the $6{,}400$ stored plans are worth about $2{,}970$ independent draws for the energy and $1{,}850$ for the cut-edge count, and the frequencies below are quoted to the precision this supports.")
rep("All four chains start from the same sector-wise construction, so we ran a fifth chain of $80{,}000$ steps from a structurally different state, an initial plan built inside the $21$ real Groups (the three single-station Groups merged into their neighbours, $18$ zones instead of five).",
    "The four chains share the sector-wise construction, so a fifth chain of $80{,}000$ steps was started from a structurally different state, an initial plan built inside the $21$ real Groups (the three single-station Groups merged into their neighbours, $18$ zones instead of five).")
P.write_text(s); print("trim pass applied")
